13:26:18 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a stereotype, and it doesn't hold up well against reality. Think about it from a base rates perspective: there are thousands of male athletes at any given school. You've probably interacted closely with a small slice of them, and the ones who fit a "dumb jock" stereotype are more memorable—confirmation bias makes them stick out while the quiet honor-roll athlete on the same team doesn't register the same way. There's also real data against this. Studies on college athletes often show athletic participation is associated with things like better time-management skills and sometimes higher-than-average GPAs compared to non-athletes at the same schools, because sports often force structure onto people who'd otherwise procrastinate more. What's actually happening with a lot of struggling student athletes isn't lack of intelligence—it's: - Time poverty (not enough hours to do both well) - Different priorities were rewarded early (some kids get praised for athletic performance from a young age and never developed strong study habits, not because they couldn't, but because nobody demanded it) - Stereotype threat—being told you're "just an athlete" can genuinely undermine performance psychologically - Some genuinely do deprioritize school because sports feel more urgent/exciting/high-stakes to them None of that is the same as innate stupidity. Your cousin is a good counterexample already—you're describing someone who's struggling, which suggests he cares enough to be stressed about it, not someone who doesn't care. If you generalize "dumb as a rock" onto every male athlete you meet going forward, you're going to consistently underestimate people and probably miss out on relationships with people who don't fit that box.
