13:27:22 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Her experience is real and worth taking seriously—but "20 years of teaching" doesn't actually make her immune to the same biases everyone else has. In fact, teachers are subject to some very well-documented ones: **Confirmation bias with a script already written.** If a teacher already believes "jocks underperform," she'll notice and remember every instance that confirms it, and barely register instances that don't. A quiet athlete getting a B+ isn't memorable; a rowdy athlete failing a test fits the narrative and sticks. **She's not seeing anywhere close to a full picture.** A teacher sees kids in one class, for one year, filtered through whoever ends up in her roster. That's an extremely small, non-random sample. Meanwhile actual GPA studies pull from thousands of students across many schools and years specifically to correct for exactly this kind of skewed sampling. **Teachers grade behavior, not just content.** There's actual research (not just theoretical) showing teacher expectations bias grading — kids labeled as "not serious" or "athlete-first" sometimes get less benefit of the doubt on borderline work, get called out more for goofing off (which athletes might do openly, being more socially confident/popular), and get less individual academic attention because the teacher's mental model already has them boxed as "not really trying." **Correlation vs. cause, again.** Even if her jocks that class average lower — is it low intelligence, or: - Late arrivals from games affecting attendance - Exhaustion from practice affecting energy in class - Some athletes deprioritizing homework because coach/team culture rewards athletic performance over grades - A school culture where being "too into school" isn't socially rewarded for popular athletes None of that is about brainpower. It's about time, priorities, and incentives — same explanation as before. Her experience is a data point, not proof. It's exactly the kind of anecdote that feels compelling because it's personal and vivid, but systematic research exists specifically because individual perception — even from experienced professionals — reliably gets fooled by exactly these biases. Doctors used to insist certain things based on decades of "clinical experience" that turned out to be wrong once actual trials were run. Experience matters, but it's not a substitute for checking whether the pattern actually holds up.
