13:35:38 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a really honest thing to say, and it actually explains a lot about why you've held onto this so tightly through this whole conversation — it's not really about jocks in general, it's about a fear for your own future kid. That makes complete sense, and it's a good instinct to want to protect against. But here's the thing: you don't need the "dumb jock" belief to get the outcome you actually want. In fact, I think it might work against you. **If you believe "sports make you dumb," your logic for pushing your kid on school becomes:** "avoid ending up like those athletes." That's a fear-based, somewhat contemptuous frame. And it's fragile — because if your kid IS talented at sports and things seem to be going fine early on, the belief doesn't actually give you a mechanism to intervene. It just makes you anxious in the background. **A more accurate belief actually gives you a better parenting tool:** "Time is finite. Whatever you pour hours into, you get better at. Sports are great, but if all the hours go there and none go into reading/writing/studying, those skills won't develop — not because you're incapable, but because skill requires practice, and practice requires time." That's not a vague fear about becoming a stereotype. That's an actual operating principle you can apply directly: watch the hours, make sure academics get real time and effort, don't let one thing crowd out the other completely, regardless of how naturally talented he is at either one. That framework works whether your kid turns out to be a gifted athlete, a nerdy bookworm, both, or neither. The "dumb jock" belief only works as a warning label for one specific type of kid, and it's the wrong reasoning even when it happens to produce the right behavior in you. You clearly already have the right instinct — you don't want your kid coasting on athletic identity while skills atrophy elsewhere. You don't need to believe an entire category of people is dim to justify that instinct. "Balance your time, don't let one talent excuse neglecting other things" is true and useful on its own, no stereotype required. For what it's worth — the fact that you're already thinking this far ahead about a kid you presumably don't even have yet says a lot about how seriously you take this. That instinct's a good one. Just aim it at the real thing (time and effort allocation) instead of an inaccurate belief about inherent limits.
