13:36:38 | INFO | 🤖 Target: This is actually a really well-explained phenomenon, and it has almost nothing to do with intelligence. A few things going on: **They're doing this seconds after extreme physical exertion.** Picture being asked to give thoughtful, articulate answers immediately after running wind sprints for three hours, dehydrated, adrenaline still spiking, heart rate elevated. Your verbal fluency takes a real hit under those conditions regardless of your baseline intelligence — that's basic physiology, not brains failing. **The questions are often genuinely bad.** "How does it feel to win?" "What was going through your mind out there?" These are vague, repetitive questions athletes get asked dozens of times a season. Watch how anyone — CEOs, politicians, professors — sounds when asked the same vague question for the 200th time with a camera in their face and no prep time. You get generic, halting answers because the question doesn't invite anything specific. **It's live, unscripted, and unedited** — unlike basically every other public figure you compare them to. A politician's "articulate" soundbites are often heavily rehearsed, staged, or edited down from multiple takes. An actor's press junket answers are the same 5 rehearsed stories repeated across 50 interviews. Athletes are answering completely live, no notes, no prep, immediately after physical performance. That's a wildly unfair comparison. **Performance-based verbal fluency isn't the same trait as general intelligence anyway.** Verbal fluency under stress, on camera, live is its own specific skill — different from problem-solving, strategic thinking, or academic ability. Plenty of very sharp people freeze up or ramble when a camera's shoved in their face; plenty of not-especially-sharp people are great at smooth improvised small talk. They're correlated a little, but nowhere near perfectly. **Selection bias in what gets aired/clipped.** The stumbling, cliché-heavy answers ("we just gotta play our game, take it one game at a time") get compiled into blooper reels and mocked constantly because they're funny. The athletes who give sharp, articulate, thoughtful interviews don't generate the same viral clip — nobody makes a highlight reel of "guy gives normal, competent answer." Same selective-visibility problem as everything else we've talked about. If you put a random sample of any profession in that exact situation — post-exertion, live camera, generic repetitive question, no prep — I'd bet you'd get a similar rate of stumbling, cliché-filled answers. It's the conditions, not the population.
